    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "SKETCHED BY I. SMITH \u0026 SON, BROOKLYN, L. I.\nEntered mending to and of Congres in the your 1850 by N. Cursin in the Clash Offer of the District Cast of Name Yout\nON STONE BY C. PARSONS\nCLIPPER SHIP «ADELAIDE»\nOFF SANDY HOOK, \"HOVE TO FOR A PILOT\"\nPUB. BY CURRIER \u0026 IVES. 152 NASSAU ST., N.Y.",
      "CLIPPER SHIP \"ADELAIDE\"\nThe front cover of this menu is a reproduction of an authentic Currier \u0026 Ives clipper ship priz\nCurrier \u0026 Ives, known as the \"Printmakers of America\", graphically preserved the glorious clipp\nship era in a magnificent series of clipper ship prints. This print, one of a series, is being reproduci\nthrough the courtesy of The Mariners Museum, Newport News, Virginia.\nThe full development of the fast-moving clipper ships coincided remarkably with the glitterir\ndiscovery of gold in America and faraway Australia. Clipper ships sailed forward in a dizzy attemp\nto combine record breaking speeds with cargo carrying capacity. Never before, nor since, has th\nworld seen anything like the sensational years that followed the Gold Rush. The clipper shij\n\"Adelaide\" was built in response to the demand for speed and to accommodate the relentless surg\nof gold-seekers. Built by A. C. Bell in 1854 in New York, she had a knife-sharp bow, long narro\nhull and magnificent display of canvas. Her speed and maneuverability, as she tore around the\nHorn, was prodigious. Right up to the beginning of the Civil War, which disrupted the trade route:\nof the \"Adelaide,\" she brought unprecedented prosperity to her East coast owners. Though her glory\nwas brief, the adventures of the \"Adelaide\" still capture the imagination of those who love the sea.\nLike Pan Am's modern Clippers* of the air, the early clipper ships traveled the lanes of com-\nmerce to all the far places of the world ... to Japan ... to Australia ... and, in the days of the\ngreat gold rush, from America's east coast, around Cape Horn, South America, to California.\nOur current equipment... the Clipper fleet of the Twentieth Century ... consists of the finest\ncommercial aircraft ever developed—aircraft that have helped us establish an unparalleled record\nfor safety, speed and service.\n\"CUPPER TRADE MARX-REG US PAT OFFICE-COPYRIGHT 1966 PAN AMEKICAN WURLU AIKWAYS IN\nLITHO IN USA-8/33-039\n2000. 056.562"
    ],
